Keyboard "Pro" Shortcuts
Stop typing everything out. Use these keys while inside CMD:

Tab: The most important key. Type the first letter of a file or folder (e.g., cd D) and hit Tab. It will auto-complete the name for you. Keep hitting Tab to cycle through options.
F3: Instantly pastes the last command you typed.
Esc: Clears the current line you are typing.
Ctrl + C: The "Panic Button." If a command is running too long (like the Matrix script), this forces it to stop immediately.


The "Process Killer"
tasklist
What it does: Shows every program running (including hidden ones), similar to Task Manager but text-based.
taskkill /f /im notepad.exe
What it does: Forcefully kills a program. Replace notepad.exe with whatever program is frozen.
When to use: When a fullscreen game crashes and you can't even open Task Manager to close it.




The "Software Installer" (New Windows 10/11 Feature)
winget search "app name" and winget install "app name"

What it does: A built-in package manager. You can install software without opening a web browser.

Try this: winget install VLC or winget install Discord.

When to use: Setting up a new PC. You can install 10 apps in one go with one command.


2. The "WiFi & Network" Masters
ipconfig /all
What it does: ipconfig gives basic info, but /all gives you everything: your MAC address, DNS servers, and detailed connection info.
When to use: When setting up advanced network filters or troubleshooting why the internet is down.
ping google.com -t
What it does: Standard ping checks connection once. Adding -t makes it ping forever until you stop it (Ctrl+C).
When to use: Crucial for testing WiFi stability. Run this and walk around your house with a laptop to see where the "dead zones" are (watch for "Request timed out").
netstat -an
What it does: Lists every active connection your computer has to the internet.
When to use: Security. If you see a connection to a suspicious IP address when you aren't doing anything, you might have malware.


The "Is My Computer Broken?" Commands
sfc /scannow
What it does: The "System File Checker." It scans Windows for corrupted or missing system files and automatically tries to repair them.
When to use: If Windows is acting weird, crashing, or giving blue screens.
Note: This usually requires running CMD as Administrator.
chkdsk /f
What it does: "Check Disk." It scans your hard drive for errors and bad sectors. The /f flag tells it to fix them.
When to use: If files are disappearing or your drive feels slow/noisy.
Warning: This might ask to restart your computer to run.